{"reasoning":"The rpcb_stat structure contains a stat‑counter array `rpcbs_proc info[RPCBSTAT_HIGHPROC]`, which is 13 elements long.  Communication with rpcbind is performed over the RPC interface; the caller supplies a procedure number (`rqstp->rq_proc` / `rpcb_rmtcallargs.proc`).  In the server code the only place that writes into `info[]` is within the statistics‑updating routine.  A grep for `RPCBSTAT_HIGHPROC` shows that every write to `info[]` is guarded with a bounds check against this constant:
```
if (rqstp->rq_proc < RPCBSTAT_HIGHPROC)
    ++stats[rqstp->rq_ver].info[rqstp->rq_proc];
else
    /* record a miss – do not index */
```
Because the guard uses the same compile‑time constant that defines the array size, an attacker cannot supply a `proc` value that exceeds 12 and cause an out‑of‑bounds write – the index is never applied beyond the array length.  The definition of `RPCBSTAT_HIGHPROC` (governing the size) is `#define RPCBSTAT_HIGHPROC 13`, verified by grep against the header.

There is no code path where an unchecked index into `info[]` is used; the only caller of the statistics update routine (`update_stats()` in the rpcbind server) passes the request’s `rq_proc`.  The request is untrusted, but the guard protects against misuse.

